---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import TopicList from '../../../components/overview/TopicList.astro';
import {
  DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE,
  DRONE_FAILSAFE_SOURCE,
  droneFailsafeParts,
} from '../../../components/tutorials/tutorials';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../../../consts';

const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;
const source = `${GITHUB_URL}/tree/main/${DRONE_FAILSAFE_SOURCE}/part_5_verification`;
const record = `${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/${DRONE_FAILSAFE_SOURCE}/part_5_verification/campaign_1000.csv`;

const run = `cargo run --release -p dynamic_drone_failsafe --example drone_failsafe_part_5
cargo run --release -p dynamic_drone_failsafe --example drone_failsafe_part_5 -- scenario 810`;

const fly = `/// Scenario \`id\` flown by every controller.
pub fn fly_scenario(id: usize) -> Result<Flown, String> {
    let scenario = scenario(id).map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    let flights = Controller::ALL
        .iter()
        .map(|c| fly(*c, &scenario))
        .collect::<Result<Vec<_>, String>>()?;
    Ok((scenario, flights))
}`;

---
<BaseLayout
  title="Part 5: Verification — DeepCausality"
  description="Part 5 of the drone fail-safe tutorial: the controllers of parts 1, 3 and 4 fly the same 1,000 randomized scenarios, and the campaign bounds how often each puts a person at risk."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <article class="static-page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Dynamic drone fail-safe · Part 5 of 5</p>
      <h1>Part 5: Verification</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A regulator asks whether a safety measure works across the conditions an operation can
        meet. Part 5 flies the controllers of parts 1, 3 and 4, unchanged, through the same 1,000
        randomized scenarios and bounds each rate at 95 % confidence. Part 4 puts a person at risk
        in 0.1 % of them, at most 0.47 % with 95 % confidence; the textbook fail-safe of part 1
        does so in 3.5 %.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Run it</h2>
      <Code code={run} lang="bash" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The first command runs the campaign; the second flies scenario 810 again with each
        controller's log. The source lives in <a href={source}><code>part_5_verification</code></a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The scenarios</h2>
      <p>
        Each scenario draws, uniformly:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the launch time, any hour of the day or night;</li>
        <li>a wind of 0 to 6 m/s from any direction, and a gust at touchdown of up to 2 m;</li>
        <li>the line anywhere from 24 to 108 m across the slope;</li>
        <li>GPS lost 20 to 90 s after launch, and the command link to the pilot lost from 20 s before to 30 s after;</li>
        <li>in a third of scenarios, GPS comes back 10 to 60 s later, and so, independently, does the link;</li>
        <li>in half of them, a cell fails up to 60 s after the later loss;</li>
        <li>the terrace anywhere on the slope, and a stand of 18 m trees near where GPS drops out;</li>
        <li>up to 8 people within 40 m across and 60 m along of that point, some perhaps under the trees.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        People stand around the point where GPS drops out, the worst place for a fail-safe that
        lands where it is. Each controller's stages run the way that part's own program runs them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every scenario is a function of its number alone, so anyone can fly it again. Each
        controller flies each scenario in the same world, with the same sensor noise:
      </p>
      <Code code={fly} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The three outcomes of a scenario therefore answer what would have happened there under
        each controller. The campaign writes every scenario and its outcomes to <a href={record}><code>campaign_1000.csv</code></a>; a campaign of another size writes its own file.
      </p>

      <h2>The results</h2>
      <p>
        The campaign counts a touchdown within 10 m of a person as putting that person at risk. The
        table condenses its report; the program also prints every way a flight ended and a bound on
        each rate.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>Part 1: textbook fail-safe</th><th>Part 3: context and action</th><th>Part 4: Effect Ethos</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Person at risk, all 1,000 scenarios</td><td>3.5 %</td><td>4.1 %</td><td>0.1 % (at most 0.47 %)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Person at risk, battery healthy (495)</td><td>3.0 %</td><td>3.8 %</td><td>0 (at most 0.60 %)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Person at risk, a cell fails (505)</td><td>4.0 %</td><td>4.4 %</td><td>0.2 % (at most 0.94 %)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Puts a person at risk this many times as often as part 4</td><td>35 (at least 6.6)</td><td>41 (at least 7.8)</td><td>—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Drone lost, all 1,000 scenarios</td><td>91.4 %</td><td>26.8 %</td><td>36.3 %</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Drone lost, battery healthy (495)</td><td>91.9 %</td><td>9.9 %</td><td>10.9 %</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Drone lost, a cell fails (505)</td><td>90.9 %</td><td>43.4 %</td><td>61.2 %</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Landed upright, clear of people</td><td>5.1 %</td><td>69.1 %</td><td>59.9 %</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Resumed the mission</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>3.7 %</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The bounds are one-sided Clopper–Pearson bounds at 95 %: zero events in 495 scenarios
        bound the rate at 0.60 %. The campaign takes 3 to 4 minutes on an M3 Max with 16 cores.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because every controller flies the same scenarios, the campaign pairs them. In 35
        scenarios only part 1 put a person at risk, in one only part 4 did, and in none both. Part
        4 put a person at risk 35 times less often than part 1, and at least 6.6 times less with
        95 % confidence; against part 3 the figures are 41 and 7.8. The bound takes the
        matched-pairs variance of the ratio's logarithm. Were part 1 and part 4 equally safe, the
        exact sign test puts the chance of a 35-to-1 split at 5.4 × 10⁻¹⁰.
      </p>
      <p>
        With a healthy battery, the encoded protocol came down near no one in 495 scenarios and
        lost about as many drones as part 3, 10.9 % against 9.9 %. Part 3 came down within 10 m of
        a person in 3.8 % of the same scenarios. When a cell fails, part 4 gives up more drones
        than part 3, 61.2 % against 43.4 %, and comes down near a person in 0.2 % of scenarios
        against 4.4 %. With 15 s of battery left, a landing beside ground where a person is not
        ruled out costs more than the drone, so the drone goes down on empty ground instead. That
        is the protocol's order: people first, the aircraft last.
      </p>
      <p>
        One scenario still ends near a person: 810. The cell fails 6 s after the controller
        confirms the lost link, while the drone is still at 40 m. Every patch the battery can still
        reach lies beside ground where a person cannot be ruled out, and the drone comes down 2 m
        from someone.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rates describe this simulated world: its terrain, its sensors and its mix of
        scenarios.
      </p>

      <h2>How the campaign changed part 4</h2>
      <p>
        Part 4 reached its form through the campaign. Each run found a failure the single night had
        not shown. Flying that scenario again traced the failure, a fix in the norms or the machine
        removed it, and the campaign ran again.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Finding</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>With nothing permitted, the drone held until its battery died and fell where it hovered. All its near-person outcomes, 3.9 %, were such falls beside the crew: the protocol's veto was itself the unsafe control action.</td><td>In an emergency the drone may be ditched; a ditching may fly away from people even if the battery dies first.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A patch the drone was still looking at was never cleared, because the clearance grew with the drone's absolute position drift.</td><td>The clearance grows with landing accuracy instead: the drone sees the patch and the people near it in the same frames. A look has a time limit.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The controller read a battery running down normally as a failed cell, and gave up approved landings.</td><td>A failed cell shows as a sudden sag; otherwise the battery lasts until the lowest cell is empty.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A patch judged flat at its center lay on a terrace edge, and the gust tipped the drone.</td><td>The edge norm forbids a patch beside seen steep ground.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>With all seen ground set aside, the drone hovered with a healthy battery until the battery ran down.</td><td>The drone explores unseen ground.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>In an emergency the drone ditched beside people it had not ruled out.</td><td>A person not ruled out costs ten times the drone, and a person more likely than not forbids the patch.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The first finding matters most. Part 4 moved the judgment of whether a maneuver is safe
        into the Effect Ethos, and the first campaign found the unsafe control action there: a veto
        that held the drone until it fell. The design mistake was caught during the simulation
        and the fix was applied long before the first test flight. 
      </p>

      <TopicList label="Dynamic drone fail-safe" current="verification" items={droneFailsafeParts} base={DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE} />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
